- in_text_citation: [(Zhao et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/901accb975508ad5b4d1f5e2638fd6e158541ab8)
- venue: Frontiers in Genetics
- citation count: 23
[method]
The paper used an E. coli complementation approach in which plasmids were constructed to express human ALKBH6 genes in an ALKB-deficient E. coli background, and the cells were then evaluated for their response to DNA alkylating agents. This is related to the proposed method because both approaches use E. coli ALKB/alkB-deficient systems and alkylating-agent challenge to probe alkylation repair, but the paper focuses on human ALKBH6 complementation rather than DNMT expression and does not describe the proposed two-way or three-way ANOVA interaction design.
[results]
The available section heading reports that human ALKBH6 genes complement ALKB deficiency in E. coli and enhance the response to DNA alkylating agents. No quantitative survival values, fold changes, p-values, or comparisons to baselines are provided in the supplied excerpt, and the heading does not specify whether the enhanced response reflects increased survival, reduced damage, or altered sensitivity.
[context]
The relevant experimental context is an E. coli ALKB-deficient strain with plasmid-based expression of human ALKBH6, exposure to DNA alkylating agents, and statistical analysis. The supplied excerpt does not specify the exact E. coli strain, plasmid details, ALKBH6 construct, alkylating agent type or concentration, treatment duration, replicate number, or statistical model.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Yamada et al., 2004-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4c3ab39f8450fb083b49b223dd8c8a763f944e00)
- venue: Genome Research
- citation count: 182
[method]
The paper used an HpaII-McrBC PCR assay to evaluate methylation status of CpG islands (CGIs) on human chromosome 21q. Genomic DNA from human peripheral blood leukocytes and placental tissues was analyzed with CGI-specific primers, and methylation-sensitive HpaII digestion was combined with McrBC-based PCR to distinguish methylated from unmethylated CCGG-containing sequences. The assay was applied to 149 CGIs and used allele-specific information, including SNPs in CGIs, to assign methylation to individual alleles; direct sequencing of HpaII-McrBC PCR products and bisulfite genomic sequencing were used to confirm methylation patterns. This method is related to the proposed research method because both rely on methylation-sensitive restriction enzyme digestion to infer 5mC status, but the paper method is locus-specific and PCR-based rather than genome-wide, and it does not use mass spectrometry to quantify total 5mC levels in E. coli DNMT-expressing cells.
[results]
The HpaII-McrBC PCR analysis covered 149 CGIs on human chromosome 21q and identified three CGIs with allele-specific methylation: CGI #112 showed maternal allele-specific methylation, CGI #59 showed mosaicism in allelic methylation status, and CGI #130 showed allele-specific, parental-origin-independent methylation. The method provided qualitative or semi-quantitative methylation status and allelic assignment rather than a genome-wide 5mC concentration, mass-spectrometry-based quantification, or survival-related measurement of DNMT-induced DNA damage.
[context]
The study was performed on human chromosome 21q CGIs in human peripheral blood leukocytes and placental tissues. Experimental setup included in silico extraction of CGIs from human chromosome 21q sequence, primer design for the CGIs, genomic DNA preparation, HpaII-McrBC PCR, identification of SNPs in CGIs, direct sequencing of PCR products, and bisulfite genomic sequencing. The context is human tissue methylation mapping and allelic methylation analysis, not heterologous E. coli expression of M.SssI or M.MpeI, and no arabinose-inducible DNMT system, DNA-damaging agent survival assay, or mass spectrometry measurement of 5mC, 5hmC, or 5fC was reported.
[end related work 1]

