- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 106
[method]
The paper included an LC-MS method to quantify 5mC in genomic DNA from C. neoformans, including experimentally evolved lines. This is related to the proposed mass-spectrometric measurement of 5mC in E. coli because both use mass spectrometry to quantify the abundance of 5mC after DNA extraction. However, the paper's method is described as an LC-MS 5mC quantification assay, not as a methylation-sensitive restriction enzyme digest assay, and it was applied to endogenous fungal methylation rather than to heterologous M.SssI or M.MpeI activity in E. coli.
[results]
The supplied paper excerpt does not provide the numerical LC-MS 5mC values, fold changes, limits of detection, or statistical significance. The associated results heading indicates that 5mC maintenance was high-fidelity during experimental evolution, but the quantitative outcome of the LC-MS method cannot be extracted from the provided text.
[context]
The method is situated in C. neoformans 5mC analysis, with related sections on experimental evolution, 5mC conservation, and Tcn3 analysis. No instrument model, nucleoside digestion protocol, internal standard, replicate number, growth conditions, or stress conditions are included in the provided excerpt. The proposed plan's E. coli arabinose-inducible DNMT system, MMS/H2O2/cisplatin treatments, and ROS/TET experiments are not part of the paper's described LC-MS method.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 3]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Schotanus et al., 2020-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/852826090a7b566dd02937d3a1dd314ed1c91f21)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 0
[method]
The paper used a sequencing-based genome-wide 5mC assay rather than methylation-sensitive restriction enzyme digestion or mass spectrometry. The relevant method is listed as Nanopore sequencing and 5mC analysis, and the full methods section list also includes bisulfite sequencing, indicating that 5mC was mapped directly or by conversion-based sequencing in C. deuterogattii. This method was applied to strains with native centromeres, neocentromeres, and engineered centromere configurations, including URA5inCEN2 and chromosome-fusion-related backgrounds, to relate 5mC distribution to CENP-A and H3K9me2 chromatin marks. It is related to the proposed method because both aim to confirm and quantify 5mC produced by methyltransferase activity, but the paper provides locus-resolved 5mC maps in a eukaryotic yeast rather than bulk 5mC quantification in E. coli by restriction digestion and mass spectrometry.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numeric 5mC values, fold changes, coverage metrics, or statistical p-values. The reported outcomes are qualitative: native centromeres were enriched for 5mC and showed correlation with H3K9me2; neocentromeres were not enriched for 5mC or H3K9me2; inactive neocentromeres lacked epigenetic memory; and CENP-A binding drifted within the ORF-free regions of CEN5 and CEN14. Thus, the method supported the conclusion that 5mC is associated with native heterochromatic centromeres rather than neocentromeres, but it does not directly quantify DNMT-dependent 5mC accumulation in E. coli or compare the activity of M.SssI and M.MpeI.
